      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most mattresses are designed around an average-weight sleeper. If you weigh more, you need a bed built for the load: a heavy-duty coil unit, dense comfort foam, a reinforced edge and a stated weight capacity from the maker.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="why-hybrids" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Heavier Sleepers Need Coils and Thicker Comfort Layers
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most mattresses are designed and rated around an average sleeper, somewhere near 130 to 230 lbs. Nothing is wrong with a heavier body on a standard bed, but the bed is working outside the range it was built for. The foam compresses further, the comfort layers bottom out, and the mattress that felt medium in the showroom feels soft and then saggy within a year or two. Heavier sleepers need a mattress engineered for more load, and in practice that almost always means a hybrid: pocketed coils for support with a thick, dense foam comfort system on top.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Pocketed Coils Carry the Load</h3>

          <p>
            Steel coils push back harder the more weight you put on them, and they recover their shape night after night in a way foam cannot match. In a pocketed coil unit, each spring sits in its own fabric sleeve and compresses on its own, so the coils under your hips work harder than the coils under your shoulders. Look for a coil unit around 8 inches tall and a maker that names the coil gauge or count. A lower gauge number means thicker steel: Big Fig, for example, lists 15-gauge coils, which is heavier than the 14.5 to 16 range most hybrids use around the core.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Comfort Layers Need Depth</h3>

          <p>
            A heavier body sinks deeper into the top layers. On a mattress with only 2 or 3 inches of foam, you push straight through the cushion and end up feeling the coils, which shows up as pressure at the shoulders and hips. That is why the beds in this guide are 11.5 to 13 inches tall. Height is not a goal in itself, but a taller mattress usually has room for more comfort foam above a full-height coil unit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Foam Density Matters More Than Thickness</h3>

          <p>
            Low-density foam is lighter and cheaper, and it breaks down faster under heavier sleepers. That breakdown is what people see as a body impression, a dip where they sleep. Makers that build for plus-size sleepers tend to call out high-density foams by name, such as Helix's SupremeSupport layer. If a listing will not say anything about foam density, assume it is ordinary.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where All-Foam Mattresses Fit</h3>

          <p>
            All-foam beds can work for heavier people if the support core is thick and dense, but they tend to sleep warmer and lose their shape sooner under higher loads. They also have weaker edges. Unless you have a specific reason, such as wanting the quietest possible bed, a hybrid is the safer choice. Our main <a href="/best-mattress/">best mattress guide</a> compares the two types in more detail for all body types.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="weight-capacity" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Weight Capacity: What the Maker's Number Actually Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            Weight capacity is the first number a heavier shopper should look for, and it is the number most listings bury or leave out. It is the maker's statement of how much weight the mattress is designed to carry before support and durability suffer. There is no industry test behind it, so treat it as a promise from that brand rather than a measured fact, and use it to rule beds out rather than to rank them.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Total Capacity vs Per-Sleeper Capacity</h3>

          <p>
            A total figure covers everything on the bed. A per-sleeper figure tells you what one side can carry. Big Fig gives both: 1,200 lbs total and 600 lbs per sleeper for the Firm queen. If a brand gives only a total, do not assume it splits evenly. A couple of 350 and 150 lbs puts most of the load on one side every night, and that side will wear first.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Capacity Changes With Size</h3>

          <p>
            The same mattress can carry different limits in different sizes. Nectar's Amazon listings give 800 lbs for the Premier Hybrid in king and 500 lbs in twin. Always check the figure for the exact size you are buying, not the headline number on the brand's home page.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Capacity Is Not a Comfort Rating</h3>

