        <div id="firmness" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Firmness for Heavier Bodies: Expect It to Feel Softer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Firmness scales are written for an average-weight sleeper. A heavier person sinks further into the same mattress, so a bed labelled medium-firm often feels medium, and medium often feels soft. As a rule of thumb, heavier sleepers should shop about one step firmer than they would expect, then use sleep position to fine-tune.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Side Sleepers</h3>

          <p>
            Side sleepers need the shoulder and hip to sink in so the spine stays level. A very firm bed pushes back at those two points, and many heavier side sleepers wake with a sore shoulder or numb arm. A medium heavy-duty hybrid like the Titan Plus Luxe is the usual answer. Our guide to the <a href="/best-mattress-for-side-sleepers/">best mattress for side sleepers</a> covers this position in depth.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Back Sleepers</h3>

          <p>
            Back sleepers need the hips held up level with the shoulders. Medium-firm is the sweet spot, and a zoned or lumbar-reinforced design can help. The Helix Plus, with its optional lumbar segment, is the most direct fit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Stomach Sleepers</h3>

          <p>
            Stomach sleeping puts the heaviest part of the body, the midsection, in the middle of the bed with nothing to hold it up but the mattress. A soft bed lets the hips sag and the lower back arch. Heavier stomach sleepers should look at firm models only, which in this guide means the Big Fig Firm.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You Have Back or Joint Pain</h3>

          <p>
            Many people with back pain prefer a medium-firm hybrid, but there is no single right firmness and a mattress is not a treatment. Talk to your doctor or physical therapist about persistent pain, and see our guide to the <a href="/best-mattress-for-back-pain/">best mattress for back pain</a> for how firmness and support interact.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="edge-support" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Edge Support: Why the Perimeter Wears First
          </h2>

          <p>
            Edge support is how well the sides of a mattress hold up when you sit or lie near them. For heavier sleepers it matters twice over. You put more weight on the edge every time you sit down to dress or get up at night, and a soft edge makes the usable sleeping surface narrower because you roll toward the middle.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sitting on the Edge</h3>

          <p>
            If you sit on the side of the bed every morning, a weak edge will compress and eventually stay compressed. A firm edge also makes standing up easier, because you push off a stable surface instead of a sinking one. That matters for anyone with sore knees.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Reinforced Coils vs Foam Rails</h3>

          <p>
            Makers reinforce edges in two main ways: a row of firmer or thicker coils around the perimeter, or a band of high-density foam. Nectar and Helix describe reinforced perimeter coils or side edges. Big Fig uses high-density foam it lists at 80 ILD. Both work. What does not work is no reinforcement at all, which is common on cheap all-foam beds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sleeping Near the Edge</h3>

          <p>
            Couples sharing a queen often end up near the edges. With good edge support, the outer 6 inches of the bed are usable rather than a slope. If you share a bed and space is tight, a king with reinforced edges is worth the extra money.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="foundation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Foundation and Bed Frame: Where Heavy-Duty Matters Most
          </h2>

          <p>
            A heavy-duty mattress on a weak frame will sag anyway, and the sag will look like a mattress fault when it is not. The foundation is also the part of the bed most likely to fail under a higher load: a cracked center slat or a bent rail can make a good mattress feel broken overnight.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Slat Spacing</h3>

          <p>
            Read the maker's rule and follow it. Helix asks for slats no more than 3.5 inches apart and says inadequate support can void the warranty. Brooklyn Bedding asks for no more than 5 inches. Wider gaps let foam and coils push down between the slats and wear unevenly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Center Support Legs</h3>

          <p>
            Any bed queen size or larger needs a center rail with at least one support leg to the floor, and heavier sleepers should look for frames with several. A metal platform frame with multiple center legs, or a dedicated heavy-duty foundation, is the safest choice. Big Fig sells a heavy-duty foundation it rates to 2,000 lbs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Old Box Springs</h3>

          <p>
            Most modern hybrids do not want a traditional sprung box spring, which flexes. If you are keeping your current base, check that it is a rigid foundation rather than springs, and check it for broken or bowed slats before the new mattress arrives.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Adjustable Bases</h3>

          <p>
            All five mattresses here are hybrids, and many hybrids work on adjustable bases, but check both the mattress maker's guidance and the base's stated weight capacity. Adjustable bases carry their own limits, and some are lower than the mattress on top of them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cooling" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Sleeping Hot: Covers, Coils and Bedding
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many heavier sleepers run warm at night. More body contact with the mattress means less air moving around you, and deeper sinkage into foam traps more heat. The mattress cannot change your body temperature, but it can avoid making things worse.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Coils Breathe Better Than Foam</h3>

          <p>
            A coil unit is mostly air, so heat has somewhere to go. That is one more reason hybrids suit heavier sleepers better than all-foam beds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Cooling Covers</h3>

          <p>
            Cooling covers use fibers that feel cool to the touch and move heat away from the surface. Titan and Helix both sell an optional GlacioTex cover, and Big Fig builds its ThermoGel top into the Firm. Treat these as a modest help rather than air conditioning.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Bedding Matters Too</h3>

          <p>
            A thick, non-breathable mattress protector can undo a cooling cover. If you add a protector, choose a breathable one; our guide to the <a href="/best-mattress-protector/">best mattress protector</a> covers the waterproof options that sleep cooler. A <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-bedroom/">bedroom dehumidifier</a> can also help in humid climates, where sticky air makes a warm bed feel worse.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="durability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. How Long a Mattress Lasts and How to Make It Last
          </h2>

          <p>
            Heavier sleepers put more wear on any mattress, so durability is part of the value. A mattress that costs more but holds its shape for eight years is cheaper per night than one that sags in three.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Rotate Every Few Months</h3>

          <p>
            Most hybrids are one-sided and should not be flipped, but they should be rotated head to foot every three to six months. That spreads the wear and slows the development of body impressions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Body Impressions and the Warranty</h3>

          <p>
            Every warranty names a depth of impression that counts as a defect, often 1 to 1.5 inches. Read that clause before buying, keep your receipt, and use the foundation the maker specifies, because an unsupported mattress can void the cover.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Use the Trial Properly</h3>

          <p>
            Most makers ask you to sleep on the bed for about 30 nights before starting a return, because your body needs time to adjust. Titan and Brooklyn Bedding both say returns can start 30 days after delivery. Note the last day of your trial, and if the bed is not right by month two, act on it. If a bed is close but slightly too firm, a <a href="/best-mattress-topper/">mattress topper</a> can soften it, though a topper cannot fix a mattress that sags. Plus-size brands run sales most months; our guide to <a href="/mattress-prices-and-deals/">mattress prices and deals</a> explains what a fair price looks like, and our <a href="/best-mattress-brands/">best mattress brands</a> roundup covers who makes what.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a Standard Mattress and Hoping It Holds Up</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A bed designed around a 200-lb sleeper will compress faster under more weight. Start with mattresses that state a weight capacity well above the combined weight of everyone using them.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading the Total Capacity as Per-Sleeper</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 1,000-lb total does not mean 500 lbs a side if one partner is much heavier. Look for a per-sleeper figure, or leave plenty of margin.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting a Heavy-Duty Mattress on a Weak Frame</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A frame without center legs, or with slats wider than the maker allows, will sag and can void the warranty. Budget for a proper foundation.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing Firmness From the Label Alone</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Heavier sleepers sink further, so medium-firm often feels medium. Shop one step firmer than you would expect, then match it to your sleep position.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the Edge</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you sit on the side of the bed to dress, a soft edge will wear first and make standing up harder. Choose reinforced coils or high-density edge foam.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

