        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mattress for heavy people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most heavier sleepers, the Brooklyn Bedding Titan Plus Luxe is the best mattress for heavy people. It is a 13-inch hybrid the maker rates to 1,000 lbs, with a medium feel that suits side and combination sleepers. Back sleepers should look at the Helix Plus, and the heaviest or stomach sleepers at the Big Fig Firm, rated to 1,200 lbs total.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What type of mattress is best for a heavy person?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A hybrid is usually the best type of mattress for a heavy person. Pocketed steel coils carry the load and recover their shape better than foam, and a thick foam comfort system on top stops you from feeling the springs. Hybrids also sleep cooler and have stronger edges than most all-foam beds, both of which matter more for heavier sleepers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How thick should a mattress be for a heavy person?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most heavier sleepers do best on a mattress 11 to 14 inches tall. That height leaves room for a full 8-inch coil unit plus enough comfort foam that you do not sink through to the springs. Thickness on its own does not guarantee support, so check the coil unit and foam density as well as the height.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is a good weight capacity for a mattress?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Look for a stated capacity comfortably above the combined weight of everyone using the bed. Standard mattresses often do not publish one. Plus-size models like the Titan Plus Luxe and Helix Plus list 1,000 lbs, and Big Fig lists 1,200 lbs total with 600 lbs per sleeper. Check the figure for the exact size you buy, since it can vary.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a firm mattress better for heavy people?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not always. Heavier sleepers do feel mattresses as softer than the label, so most should shop slightly firmer than average. But side sleepers still need some give at the shoulder and hip, and a very firm bed can create pressure points. Firm suits heavier stomach sleepers; medium to medium-firm suits most side and back sleepers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do heavy people need a special bed frame?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Heavier sleepers need a sturdy frame more than a special one. Choose a metal platform or foundation with a center rail and several legs to the floor, and follow the mattress maker's slat-spacing rule, often 3.5 to 5 inches. Big Fig sells a heavy-duty foundation it rates to 2,000 lbs. A weak base can sag and void the mattress warranty.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a mattress last for a heavy person?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends heavily on construction. A hybrid built for plus-size sleepers, with dense foams and a heavy-duty coil unit, can last many years with regular rotation. A standard bed-in-a-box may show body impressions within a few years under higher loads. Rotate it every three to six months and keep the receipt for any warranty claim.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

